Creates an empty map with no allocated buckets.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
```

Drops all occupied entries but keeps the bucket allocation for reuse.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.insert(1, 10);  
map.clear();  
```

Returns `true` if `key` is present in the map.  

This probes directly without materializing an intermediate `Option<&V>`.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.insert(1, 10);  
let found: boolean = map.contains(&1);  
```

Returns an immutable reference to the value for `key`, if present.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.insert(7, 99);  
let value: Option<&i32> = map.get(&7);  
```

Returns a mutable reference to the value for `key`, if present.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.insert(7, 99);  
let value: Option<&mut i32> = map.getMut(&7);  
```

Removes `key` from the map and returns its previous value, if present.  

Removal leaves a tombstone bucket behind so probe chains remain valid.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.insert(1, 10);  
let removed: Option<i32> = map.remove(&1);  
```

Ensures enough buckets exist to insert `additional` more entries without  
violating the current load-factor policy.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.reserve(32);  
```

Creates an empty set with no allocated buckets.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  

let set: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new<i32>();  
```

Removes all values while preserving the current bucket allocation.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  

let mut set: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new<i32>();  
set.insert(1);  
set.clear();  
```

Returns `true` if `value` is already present.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  

let mut set: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new<i32>();  
set.insert(7);  
let found: boolean = set.contains(&7);  
```

Removes `value` from the set.  

Returns `true` when the value existed and `false` otherwise.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  

let mut set: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new<i32>();  
set.insert(7);  
let removed: boolean = set.remove(&7);  
```

Ensures the set can accommodate `additional` more elements without  
immediate growth.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  

let mut set: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new<i32>();  
set.reserve(32);  
```

items.doc:
Returns an owned copy of the environment variable when it is present.  

The returned string is copied from host-owned storage before it leaves this  
function. A present but empty variable returns `Option::SOME("")`; only an  
absent variable returns `Option::NONE`.  

# Arguments  

- `name`: environment variable name to query.  

# Returns  

- `Option::SOME(value)` when the variable exists. `value` is an owned copy.  
- `Option::NONE` when the variable is absent.  

# Invariants  

- Empty variables are still present: `NAME=` returns `Option::SOME("")`.  
- The returned `String` does not borrow from the host environment.  
- Later `Env::set` or `Env::unset` calls do not invalidate earlier results.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Env from "std::env";  
import Option from "std::option";  

Env::set("IGNIS_MODE", "debug");  
match (Env::get("IGNIS_MODE")) {  
  Option::SOME(value) -> {  
    // value is an owned String containing "debug".  
  },  
  Option::NONE -> {},  
};  
```

Prints `message` to stderr without a trailing newline.  

# Arguments  

* `message` - The String to print (consumed/moved).  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Io from "std::io";  
import String from "std::string";  

Io::eprint(String::create("warning: "));  
Io::eprintln(String::create("low disk space"));  
```

Prints `message` to stderr followed by a newline (`\n`).  

Use this for error messages, warnings, and diagnostic output  
that should not mix with normal stdout.  

# Arguments  

* `message` - The String to print (consumed/moved).  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Io from "std::io";  
import String from "std::string";  

Io::eprintln(String::create("error: file not found"));  
Io::eprintln("error from str");  
```

Prints `message` to stdout without a trailing newline.  

Useful for building up output incrementally or printing prompts.  

# Arguments  

* `message` - The String to print (consumed/moved).  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Io from "std::io";  
import String from "std::string";  

Io::print(String::create("Enter name: "));  
```

Prints `message` to stdout followed by a newline (`\n`).  

This is the most common way to produce output. The String is  
moved into the function and dropped after printing.  

# Arguments  

* `message` - The String to print (consumed/moved).  

# Example  

```ignis  
